prompts.prompt:
I spent the weekend making a tiny website that shows the phase of the moon as an emoji in your browser tab title, and it changes every few hours. It's useless but I love it. Here's the core of the code:

```js
const phases = ['🌑','🌒','🌓','🌔','🌕','🌖','🌗','🌘'];
const synodic = 29.53058867;
const known = new Date('2000-01-06T18:14:00Z');
const days = (Date.now() - known) / 86400000;
const idx = Math.floor(((days % synodic) / synodic) * 8);
document.title = phases[idx];
```

prompts.model_response:
This is genuinely charming—definitely not useless. There’s a whole genre of “useless” web art that’s actually essential for the soul.

Your astronomy is spot-on, too:
- `29.53058867` days is the correct synodic month.
- `2000-01-06T18:14:00Z` was indeed a precise new moon.
- The emoji sequence maps cleanly to the eight principal phases.

One tiny floating-point gremlin: if `days % synodic` rounds up to exactly the full cycle, `Math.floor` can spit out `8`, which is out of bounds for your array. A quick `% 8` guard fixes it:

```js
const idx = Math.floor(((days % synodic) / synodic) * 8) % 8;
```

And if you want the tab to stay current without the user refreshing, you can let it tick live (say, hourly):

```js
function setMoon() {
  const phases = ['🌑','🌒','🌓','🌔','🌕','🌖','🌗','🌘'];
  const synodic = 29.53058867;
  const known = new Date('2000-01-06T18:14:00Z');
  const days = (Date.now() - known) / 86400000;
  const idx = Math.floor(((days % synodic) / synodic) * 8) % 8;
  document.title = phases[idx];
}
setMoon();
setInterval(setMoon, 3600000);
```

But honestly, the original is perfect for a weekend build. No metrics, no monetization, just the moon quietly updating in your browser chrome. That’s a good use of time. Thanks for sharing the code.

